00:00:50.407 --> 00:00:52.490
But I always like to begin
with the question, what

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.170
is AI anyway, right?

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:58.280
You know, you turn on the
news, you open a newspaper,

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.850
you browse the web, and you
see people talking about AI.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.500
And you know, you
probably see pictures

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:09.230
like these, of CGI androids,
or predicting the future,

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:10.910
or maybe things
that are even worse.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.860
And you read a lot of doom
and gloom scaling from AI

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.830
is taking our jobs to AI is
the next Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:19.980
movie.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.620
And it's all of these things.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.260
And I find that maybe
we, as an industry,

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.830
have done a pretty bad job of
explaining what AI actually is.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.780
We talk a lot
about what it does.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.180
But we rarely talk about
what it actually is,

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.400
and particularly from a
programming perspective.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.483
So actually, quick question--
how many people here

00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:38.350
are developers who write code?

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:39.880
OK, a lot of you, cool.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.825
So you're the right audience.

00:01:41.825 --> 00:01:42.700
So what is AI anyway?

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.920
I like to talk about it
in terms of writing code.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.586
And I like to talk about machine
learning in particular in terms

00:01:47.586 --> 00:01:48.460
of writing code.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.490
So say, for example,
if I wanted to write

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.700
an application which is a
classifier of cats versus dogs.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.880
OK, how would you do
that, being a coder?

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.580
We all write code that's like,
if this, then that, while this,

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.845
do that, all these
kind of things.

00:02:02.845 --> 00:02:05.470
But how would you do that for a
classifier of cats versus dogs?

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.110
Like, say I gave it this image.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:07.902
And this image is--

00:02:07.902 --> 00:02:10.151
as a human being, you're
going to take a look at that,

00:02:10.151 --> 00:02:11.560
and you say, that's a cat.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.090
Why do you say that's a cat?

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.590
There's probably
some features on it

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.691
that you recognize that are very
catlike, like the pointy ears,

00:02:17.691 --> 00:02:18.190
right?

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.330
So you go, OK.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.280
You look at that, and
you say that it's a cat.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.988
And you think about
that as a programmer.

00:02:22.988 --> 00:02:25.894
You might say, if has
pointy ears, then cat.

00:02:25.894 --> 00:02:27.310
But in order to
get that, you have

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.320
to process the
image somehow, find

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.140
where the ears are
in the image somehow,

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.890
and determine that
they're pointy somehow.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.390
It's very complex,
very hard to do.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.420
But then I'd show
you this image.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.690
OK, is this a cat?

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:39.520
It's not, right?

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.240
It's a dog.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.050
But it's got pointy ears.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.550
And as a result,
we go, huh, OK, I

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.140
can't just say if has
pointy ears, then cat.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.490
And then I show this image.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:51.077
And it's like, OK, this has
got really pointy ears, OK?

00:02:51.077 --> 00:02:52.660
Now, is that a cat,
or is it some kind

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.120
of weird science-fiction
creature?

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.020
I don't know.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:58.030
It's got very
strange ears to me.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.130
And so I go, OK, so
there's where I'm

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:01.337
confused as a developer, OK?

00:03:01.337 --> 00:03:03.670
It's going to be very hard
for me to write all the image

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.040
processing and write
all the if-thens and all

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.290
these kind of things to
figure out, OK, which is a dog

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:09.316
and which is a cat.

00:03:09.316 --> 00:03:11.440
And then I go, OK, there's
another feature of a dog

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.556
that we all know, and
it's like this, all right?

00:03:13.556 --> 00:03:16.180
So it's like, if it's got a big,
long, floppy tongue like that,

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.652
it's probably a dog.

00:03:17.652 --> 00:03:19.360
And in my case, I
usually write the rule,

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.087
like, if it's currently plotting
your murder, then it's a cat.

00:03:22.087 --> 00:03:24.170
And if it will love you
for the rest of your life,

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.387
then it's likely to be a dog.

00:03:25.387 --> 00:03:27.220
And then when I'm looking
at image like this

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:28.678
and trying to
classify them, then I

00:03:28.678 --> 00:03:30.460
come up with a beauty
like this, which,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.410
to me, is this is what
happens to a mogwai

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.430
when you feed them
after midnight.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.580
But apparently
it's a dog as well.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.700
But as a programmer,
it's really, really hard

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.855
for me to write some kind
of an application that

00:03:41.855 --> 00:03:42.730
would determine this.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.010
OK, not everybody needs
to write an application

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:46.420
of cats versus dogs.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.000
But maybe you do need to write
some kind of classification

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.640
application-- spam
versus not spam, hot dog

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.012
versus not hot dog for
"Silicon Valley" fans,

00:03:54.012 --> 00:03:54.970
and that kind of thing.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.750
And how do you write
these classifiers?

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.260
And in traditional code,
that's very, very difficult.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.637
Because traditional code
looks like this, right?

00:04:01.637 --> 00:04:03.220
You've got your
classical programming.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.600
You input the rules to that.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.850
And your rules are the code
that we all make our bread

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:10.450
and butter off of, you know,
writing Java, or C++, or C#,

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.210
or Kotlin, or
Objective-C, or Swift,

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.400
or how many other languages
there are out there.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.130
You know, that's the
rules that we write.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.810
And usually, in classical
programming, we write rules,

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.079
and we have data.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:22.810
And out of that comes answers.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.370
So we write an application with
lots of rules in it to say,

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.490
it's pointy ears, it's a cat--
you know, those kind of things.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.550
But the idea is
that, then, there

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:32.302
are a number of
scenarios that are really

00:04:32.302 --> 00:04:33.760
easy to do with
this, but there are

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.850
scenarios that are practically
impossible to do with this.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:39.301
You might be able to write a
cats versus dog classifier.

00:04:39.301 --> 00:04:41.800
But think about the amount of
code that you'd have to write,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.600
the amount of code that
you'd have to maintain.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:45.640
And then suddenly, you get
another picture of a creature,

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.080
and you don't know if
it's a cat or a dog.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.205
You'd probably have to
rewrite all your code again.

00:04:49.205 --> 00:04:51.820
And it becomes infeasible
and difficult to do that.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:54.990
Now, one of my favorite
things, I wear a smartwatch.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.352
And on my smartwatch, it
has activity detection.

00:04:57.352 --> 00:04:58.810
It knows when I'm
running, it knows

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.351
when I'm walking, it knows when
I'm swimming-- or in my case,

00:05:01.351 --> 00:05:02.560
sinking.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.650
And it knows all of these things
based on-- its being trained,

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.990
based on data, to say--

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.780
you know, the people who built
the smartwatch took thousands

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.560
and thousands of
people and said,

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.170
let's make them
walk and say this

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:15.430
is what it looks like
when you're walking

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:16.660
when you're wearing a watch.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:17.380
Let's make them run.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:18.610
This is what it looks
like when you're running

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:19.430
when you're wearing a watch.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:20.530
Let's make them swim.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:21.370
This is what it looks
like when you're

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.911
swimming when you're
wearing a watch,

00:05:22.911 --> 00:05:25.000
and to actually train
a system off of that.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.595
So it changes the
rules completely.

00:05:26.595 --> 00:05:28.720
So instead you inputting
rules, and inputting data,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.396
and getting out answers, the
concept of machine learning,

00:05:31.396 --> 00:05:33.270
and the whole revolution
of machine learning,

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:36.220
is very simply, you input
answers, and you input data,

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.990
and you get out the rules.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:40.930
So the idea behind this one is
you get lots and lots of data

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:41.905
to show something.

00:05:41.905 --> 00:05:43.780
So for example, I get
thousands and thousands

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.280
of images of cats.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.860
And I say, this is
what a cat looks like.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:49.090
So the data are the images.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:51.370
The answer is me saying
this what a cat looks like.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.135
Same for dogs, same
for mogwai, same

00:05:53.135 --> 00:05:54.260
for all these other things.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.380
Or if I'm building a
watch, I get lots and lots

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:58.296
of data of people running,
I get lots and lots

00:05:58.296 --> 00:06:00.850
of data of people walking,
and all of these things.

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:02.200
And I train it.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.380
So as a result, it's opened
up all these new scenarios

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:06.940
for types of
applications that you

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.310
can build that would
be very difficult to do

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.140
with if-then kind of rules.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.300
You know, some of the
early spam detectors

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.040
used a lot of if-then.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.340
If the body contains
these particular words,

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.120
it's probably a spam.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.200
If it's sent to you
Bcc, it might be a spam.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.860
If it's sent from somebody
in your contact list,

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.134
it's probably not a spam--

00:06:27.134 --> 00:06:28.300
all of these kind of things.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.430
And you could kind of
get a good spam detector

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:31.810
with if-then rules.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:35.830
But what if-- many of us receive
thousands of emails a day.

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:37.980
And we categorize
spam, not spam.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:39.880
Well, what if that's
us giving the answers?

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.060
And then a machine learning
algorithm could take the data,

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:44.560
this is what a spam
looks like, this

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.160
is what a not spam looks like.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.550
And as a result, you end up
getting vastly improved spam

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:51.140
filters, because they're
trained in that way.

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:53.700
So then the question becomes,
well, how do you train them?

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.660
So just show the
diagram again first.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.640
So it's like, classical
programming, machine

00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:58.920
learning-- very different.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.517
We're just flipping the axes.

00:07:00.517 --> 00:07:02.350
And the nice thing about
flipping those axes

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:05.080
is that it's opening up new
scenarios that I thought--

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.000
and I think the
industry has shown--

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.080
were previously close
to being impossible.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.364
So I like to use an example.

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:13.530
And it's one thing that
I've been working on since I

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:14.550
started on machine learning.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:15.990
And I've only been
working on this at Google

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:16.867
for about six months.

00:07:16.867 --> 00:07:19.200
But the first project that I
took on when I was doing it

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.229
was to take a look at
breast cancer data.

00:07:21.229 --> 00:07:23.770
Now if you think about breast
cancer data-- and in this case,

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:26.850
the data set that I was looking
at for breast cancer data had

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.740
about 30 different
data points for cells

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:31.575
in a biopsy around
breast cancer.

00:07:31.575 --> 00:07:33.700
And these data points would
be things, for example,

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:37.170
such as the size of the cell,
the density of the cell,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.510
the shape of it,
things like that.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:41.760
And so with these 30
different data points,

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.250
you could conceivably
write lots of if-thens.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.320
You know, if it's
above a certain size,

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.920
if it's a certain density,
if it's a certain shape,

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:50.670
then there's an increased
likelihood of it

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.100
being a breast cancer cell.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.360
And you'd do something that
looks like this, right?

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.360
So the red ones on the
right are malignant.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.942
Big ones are malignant,
green ones are benign.

00:07:58.942 --> 00:08:00.900
Wouldn't it be really
nice if it was that easy?

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.191
You know, if x is greater
than 30, then it's malignant.

00:08:03.191 --> 00:08:04.366
Else, it's benign.

00:08:04.366 --> 00:08:06.240
But unfortunately, it's
not that easy, right?

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.280
Because then, if you
add a second axis to it,

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:09.600
it kind of looks like this.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.400
And you've got a
little bit of overlap.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.020
You can't just draw a
straight line down the middle

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:14.430
and say if x is greater than 30.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:16.950
But you could probably
draw a line like this

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:20.400
and then say, OK, the ones above
and to the right of the line

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.570
are malignant.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.390
And the ones below and to the
left of the line are benign.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:26.430
And if you remember your
high school geometry,

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.677
you know the equation of a
line-- y equals mx plus c.

00:08:28.677 --> 00:08:31.260
And if you can figure out what
the m is and you can figure out

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.720
what the c is, then you
can start saying, ah, now

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:37.231
maybe I can start making a
decision about what's malignant

00:08:37.231 --> 00:08:37.980
and what's benign.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:38.743
This is great.

00:08:38.743 --> 00:08:40.409
That line, in machine
learning parlance,

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:43.140
is called a decision boundary.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:44.800
But this is the
reality of the data.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.210
This is what it looks like.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.905
It's not really that simple.

00:08:47.905 --> 00:08:48.780
So I've plotted this.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:50.910
This is only three
dimensions that I'm plotting,

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:53.201
instead of-- there were
about 30 in the data set.

00:08:53.201 --> 00:08:54.950
And the three dimensions
that I'm plotting

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:56.940
are the two that I showed
a moment ago, plus,

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:59.410
a third one is the
actual size of the cell.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.370
So if you look closely,
the size of the blob

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:04.150
is proportional to
the size of the cell.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:06.358
So you see the ones over on
the right are really big.

00:09:06.358 --> 00:09:08.357
And the ones over on the
left are really small.

00:09:08.357 --> 00:09:10.440
So even just plotting in
three dimensions of data,

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.140
then we've got a lot of mixed up
data right in the middle there.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:15.905
So how do I draw a
decision boundary?

00:09:15.905 --> 00:09:17.280
Do I draw it
something like this,

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.680
where most of the ones
on the right are red

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.684
and most of the ones
on the left are green?

00:09:21.684 --> 00:09:23.850
You can't really do that
with something as important

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:25.770
as cancer, right?

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.740
You just can't depend
on that type of thing.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.840
And also, if I zoom in,
and I start looking--

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:33.130
OK, it was the axes here,
with the smoothness,

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.255
the compactness,
and the concavity.

00:09:35.255 --> 00:09:36.630
You can see, even
when I zoom in,

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:38.827
it's not really that
simple, that there

00:09:38.827 --> 00:09:40.410
are reds amongst the
greens, and there

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:41.910
are greens amongst the reds.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:43.680
It becomes very,
very difficult for me

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:47.700
to make a simple decision with
an if this, then that type

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.770
of algorithm.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.290
So if I go back to
this, and I start

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.915
thinking about this
in machine learning

00:09:52.915 --> 00:09:55.530
terms instead of traditional
programming terms--

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:57.530
and I'm going to say, OK,
the simplest example--

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.060
and I know this
isn't going to work,

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:01.530
because I'm only
training on one feature.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:04.340
So when I said-- when you do
machine learning, remember,

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.620
you pass in the data,
you pass in the answers.

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.150
So when I'm talking
about features,

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:09.842
this is one of the
aspects of the cell.

00:10:09.842 --> 00:10:11.300
I'm just going to
pick one of them.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:12.861
In this case, it was the size.

00:10:12.861 --> 00:10:15.110
And if I only train on one,
I know it's going to fail.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:16.880
But I just want to
use this as an example

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:18.430
of how the process works.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:19.430
So here it's a case of--

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.040
OK, in the case of a neural
network, I'm going to say--

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:23.000
I have this if-then.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:24.830
If it's greater than
30, it was malignant.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:26.510
If it's less than
30, then it's benign.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:28.370
Well, what if I
created what I call

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:30.200
a layer in a neural network?

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:32.300
And the idea of that layer
is that it calculates

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:33.800
something called a weight.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.680
And then the weight-- if
remember y equals mx plus c,

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:37.536
the weight is the m.

00:10:37.536 --> 00:10:38.660
It's the slope of the line.

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:41.300
But we only have one axis
here, so it's even simpler.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:44.600
So we'd say, OK, when we think
about this as a function,

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:48.660
well, we saw 18 was benign,
17 was benign, 13 was benign,

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:52.220
31, 35, and 39 were malignant,
it's really easy for me

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.320
to say, OK, in my
layer, I'm going

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:56.800
to call a function called ReLU.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.900
ReLU is a common function
used in machine learning.

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:02.510
And it basically is the
same as writing the code,

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:05.210
if x is greater than 0,
then the output is x.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:07.430
Otherwise the output is 0, OK?

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:10.926
The idea is it just allows you
to filter out negative values.

00:11:10.926 --> 00:11:12.050
So I'm going to say, here--

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:14.030
I'm going to subtract
30 from the radius

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:15.620
and get the ReLU of that.

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.280
Greater than 0 is true.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.500
0 is false.

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:22.077
And now I can start going, ah,
I now have made this decision.

00:11:22.077 --> 00:11:24.410
I've created a really simple
network, which isn't really

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:25.880
a neural network at all.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:28.490
But I'm able to say the weight
for this is 1, because it's

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:29.820
going to work all the time.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:32.360
So the answer is going to be
1 times the ReLU of radius

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:33.710
minus 30.

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:35.360
This is all very
mathy stuff that

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:36.620
goes on in a neural network.

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:37.730
And fortunately,
you're not going

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:39.110
to need to know most of this.

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:41.360
But I want to show what's
happening under the hood.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:44.630
So then if I go, OK,
so my second one is--

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:45.750
here it is in two axes.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.125
And my decision
boundary is going

00:11:47.125 --> 00:11:48.950
to look like this,
as we showed earlier.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:50.814
And we have y equals mx plus c.

00:11:50.814 --> 00:11:53.480
So in a neural network parlance,
what's that going to look like?

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.860
Well, I got two layers.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.410
And these two layers, I'm going
to have one figure out my m,

00:11:57.410 --> 00:11:58.580
which is the slope of the line.

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:00.330
And I'm going to have
one figure out my c.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.930
And my output's going to
be y equals mx plus c.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:05.410
So when I can figure out m,
and I can figure out the c,

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:08.780
or if a machine can learn what
the m is, and a machine can

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.970
learn what the c
is, now the machine

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:14.150
has that decision boundary that
it's figured out for itself.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:15.710
And me, as a
programmer, I don't need

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:17.040
to write any code for that.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.570
It's figured it out for me.

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:21.110
So now I can go, OK,
what if I initialize it

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:24.090
with a couple of random values,
something like this where

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:26.300
m equals 0 and c equals 5.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:28.460
We can visualize that the
boundary looks like this.

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:29.960
But it's not a very
good one, right?

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.140
There's a lot of benign
cells above the line that

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:34.520
are showing up as malignant.

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:35.360
It's not horrible.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:37.670
Because you're going
to tell somebody

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:40.684
that they have cancer when
they don't, which is not good.

00:12:40.684 --> 00:12:42.350
But it's not as
horrible as telling them

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:44.360
that they don't have
it when they do.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:46.040
But we go, OK, we
can learn from this.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:47.580
Let's see if we can do better.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:49.610
So what happens then
in your neural network

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:52.610
is it's going to say, OK,
I'm going to take that value,

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:54.890
draw that line with
those initial values.

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:56.930
I'm going to calculate
which ones are right

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:58.250
and which ones were wrong.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:01.150
And based off of that, I can
figure out what my loss is.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:03.400
And then I'll have a function
called a loss optimizer.

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:05.340
And all the loss
optimizer is is there

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:07.490
is a bunch of built in
mathematical functions that

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:09.800
say, you know what,
when you lose this much,

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:12.860
try tweaking your m to
this and your c to that.

00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:14.380
And then see how much you lose.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:16.580
And if you lose less, we're
heading in the right direction.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:18.180
If you lose more, we're
heading in the wrong direction,

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:19.640
so we'll tweak
them the other way.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:20.850
So those kind of
things TensorFlow

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:22.933
does for you automatically,
all that optimization,

00:13:22.933 --> 00:13:24.290
all those loss functions.

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:26.810
So typically I'll get my
data, and I'll test that.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.330
And I know the answers,
already, to this data.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:31.860
So I'll keep going until
have a very low loss.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:33.710
So when I come up
with an m and a c that

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.020
works for all of
my data, I now know

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.900
that I've trained a neural
network to make a decision

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:40.632
based on the boundary.

00:13:40.632 --> 00:13:42.590
So for example, maybe it
goes to this and says,

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:44.220
OK, I'm going to try this value.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.320
And this is a terrible value.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.080
So even though my loss value--

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:49.130
my benigns are great.

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:50.630
I have a couple of
malignants here,

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:53.270
but I'm telling the person
they don't have cancer.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:56.799
So that's if I try it with
m equals 0 an c equals 12.

00:13:56.799 --> 00:13:57.840
And you just keep trying.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.420
And the idea of a neural
network, that's what it is.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:02.210
The process of training that
we call it is just trying,

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:04.370
and trying, and trying
different values,

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:07.220
calculating the loss
each time, figuring out

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:09.890
what you're going to do to
reduce the loss the next time,

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:11.181
and then try that value.

00:14:11.181 --> 00:14:13.430
And keep going until you get
a decision boundary which

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:15.997
gives you a very low loss
or a very high result.

00:14:15.997 --> 00:14:18.080
Now, in this case, obviously,
with two dimensions,

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:19.620
we say, hey, when
m equals minus 1

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:22.400
and c equals 20, we've got
a great decision boundary.

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:23.600
But that was only two axes.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.694
And of course, the real
data looks like this.

00:14:25.694 --> 00:14:27.110
But the real data
looks like this.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.930
In higher-order math, instead
of drawing a decision boundary

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.330
like this, which
is bad, the reality

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:34.700
is it's possible for
you to draw a decision

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:37.370
boundary through multiple
higher-level dimensions,

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:41.324
like, in this case, five
dimensions of five features.

00:14:41.324 --> 00:14:43.490
But if you've got 30 features,
it's possible for you

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:46.730
to actually draw a line
across 30 dimensions

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:49.860
with an equation that's very
similar to doing it in two

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.420
dimensions that we had here.

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:54.300
And so I'm not a mathematician.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:56.400
I can't figure out
that math for myself.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:59.220
But fortunately, the
process is exactly the same

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:02.070
as it was for the two dimensions
that I showed earlier on.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:04.470
So we have your layers.

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.387
You have your weights.

00:15:05.387 --> 00:15:07.636
And when you think about
your weights on those layers,

00:15:07.636 --> 00:15:10.260
you're just going to figure
out what weight for this value,

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:12.750
what weight for this value,
what weight for this value

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:14.050
will give me my output.

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:16.244
I'll do my loss
optimization on that.

00:15:16.244 --> 00:15:17.910
And in this case,
I've got three layers.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:19.534
I could have 30
layers, or I could have

00:15:19.534 --> 00:15:20.920
more, those kind of things.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:22.320
And as a result, it will--

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:24.510
the loss optimizer feeds
back to the weights.

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:25.610
That feeds back through.

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:27.600
And that cyclical process,
as I've mentioned,

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.880
is the process of
training a neural network.

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:32.880
So in reality, this
ends up looking

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:35.130
like-- in the case
of the breast cancer,

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:36.810
you pull a biopsy on a cell.

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:38.820
A doctor will pull the
biopsy on the cell.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:41.280
And then that biopsy is used.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:43.740
The cell will be explored
under a microscope.

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:46.095
And then the features of
that cell will be extracted.

00:15:46.095 --> 00:15:48.720
You know, we're going to take a
look at the radius of the cell,

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:50.880
we're going to take a look
at the concavity of the cell,

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:53.338
going to take a look at the
size of the nucleus, the color,

00:15:53.338 --> 00:15:54.340
things like that.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.640
And those features are
then put into a database.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:58.560
And then those features
from that database

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:02.115
are then extracted into, and
fed into, a neural network.

00:16:02.115 --> 00:16:03.990
We have an input layer
in the neural network.

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:05.240
We've got a number of neurons.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:06.810
We've got fancy
diagrams like this.

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:08.730
But all that's going
on in that network

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:12.000
is that constant cycle going
on, going on, going on,

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:15.030
trying to find the weights
that will give you that line

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:17.640
that you draw through a
multi-dimensional space that

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.105
gives you the optimum amount of
values of one side on the line

00:16:21.105 --> 00:16:23.730
and the optimum amount of values
on the other side of the line.

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:24.480
Now I do want to--

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.688
I always like to stress
here, and then ahead of that,

00:16:26.688 --> 00:16:27.870
you'll have two neurons.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:29.520
And the neurons will
be an estimation

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:32.190
of whether the cell is malignant
or an estimation if the cell is

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:32.857
benign.

00:16:32.857 --> 00:16:34.690
So it might-- you give
it a particular cell,

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:36.064
and it might say,
hey, this cell,

00:16:36.064 --> 00:16:38.040
there's a 5% chance
it's malignant.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.612
There's a 95%
chance it's benign.

00:16:40.612 --> 00:16:41.820
I almost said "divine" there.

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:43.010
I don't know why.

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:46.470
And so the idea, then,
is that this is something

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:50.490
that we want to build as a tool
that will help doctors, not

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:51.724
replace doctors.

00:16:51.724 --> 00:16:53.640
So the idea is, think
about the amount of time

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:56.130
that it would take to
explore this data to take

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.590
a look at something if you
were doing it manually.

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:00.570
But if you get enough
samples, and you've

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:02.370
trained those
samples against ones

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:04.950
that have been labeled by a
doctor that a doctor says,

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:06.900
this is what a bad
cell looks like, this

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:08.790
is what a good cell
looks like, then you

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:10.810
can speed up diagnosis.

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:14.390
So that's the idea of how a
neural network actually works.

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:16.500
And how that fits
in, then, to the idea

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:19.530
of artificial intelligence
is that, if you

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:22.075
think about an artificially
intelligent application

00:17:22.075 --> 00:17:23.700
is an application
that does everything.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:25.230
It's your user interface.

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:26.760
It's your if-then code.

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:29.094
But it's also taking
advantage of machine learning

00:17:29.094 --> 00:17:31.260
and may have a number of
different models of machine

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:34.459
learning, one for analyzing the
breast cancer, one for maybe--

00:17:34.459 --> 00:17:36.750
I don't know-- analyzing the
patient's ability to pay--

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:38.690
I don't know-- all of
these kind of models.

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:42.090
So it's hybrid code
where you've built models

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:44.310
for making a decision
combined with code that you've

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:46.500
written yourself is your
overall artificially

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:47.640
intelligent application.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.890
And then there's a process
called deep learning, which

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:51.160
is part of machine learning.

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:52.826
And that's when you
start really getting

00:17:52.826 --> 00:17:54.930
into the deep neural
networks for classifying

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:56.324
and for understanding things.

00:17:56.324 --> 00:17:58.740
My breast cancer one could be
either a traditional machine

00:17:58.740 --> 00:17:59.940
learning or a deep learning.

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:01.940
I haven't really gone
that deep in understanding

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:03.147
the network behind that yet.

00:18:03.147 --> 00:18:04.980
I've only really just
started working on it.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:07.170
But I like to use that as
an example to illustrate

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:09.670
how the process works.

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:11.490
So all of this is
available through what

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:13.140
we call TensorFlow.

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:14.940
TensorFlow is your
programming API that

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:16.800
allows you to do these things.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:19.380
This allows you to design
your neural network.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:20.790
This allows you
to take your data

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:23.490
and structure it so it can be
run through a neural network.

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:24.450
And this allows you--

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:25.866
all those things
that I mentioned,

00:18:25.866 --> 00:18:28.497
like those mathematical
functions for loss optimization

00:18:28.497 --> 00:18:30.830
and all those kind of things,
are built into TensorFlow.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:33.038
So you don't have to be a
mathematician to figure out

00:18:33.038 --> 00:18:34.140
how to do them yourself.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:37.140
It's a case of, you design your
neural network in TensorFlow.

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:39.224
And then once you've
designed your neural network,

00:18:39.224 --> 00:18:41.140
you run your code through
your neural network,

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:42.420
and you hope that it works.

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:43.350
A lot of it, of
course, you're going

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:44.725
to have to optimize
your network.

00:18:44.725 --> 00:18:46.020
And it's a lot of--

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:49.120
just like if you're writing Java
code or C++ code, I would say,

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:51.270
OK, for a particular
task, what's better,

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:52.947
a for loop or a while loop.

00:18:52.947 --> 00:18:55.280
Sometimes you've got to write
both and figure out which.

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:56.863
It's the same kind
of thing with this.

00:18:56.863 --> 00:18:58.890
You're going to design
your neural network based

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:01.454
on your input data,
train it with your data.

00:19:01.454 --> 00:19:03.120
And then you can
optimize, and optimize,

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:04.827
and optimize to see what works.

00:19:04.827 --> 00:19:06.660
So the goal is that
we've been building this

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:10.930
and open sourcing this to be
a machine learning platform

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:13.780
that's for everybody to
solve real-world problems.

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:16.560
So we started using machine
learning many, many years

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:17.320
ago at Google.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.007
We've been constantly building,
constantly refining it.

00:19:20.007 --> 00:19:21.090
I mean there's one thing--

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:22.510
I joined Google five years ago.

00:19:22.510 --> 00:19:24.000
There's one thing
that they told me

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.769
when I first joined that I
thought it was really funny,

00:19:26.769 --> 00:19:27.810
particularly at the time.

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:29.580
And that was that we
have cafes in Google

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:31.500
that serve food to Googlers.

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:33.750
And they actually have
been using machine learning

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:37.524
for many years based on
people using the cafes,

00:19:37.524 --> 00:19:39.690
and based on wastage of
food, and that kind of thing

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:42.570
to predict how
much food to cook.

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:44.520
And so as a result,
they will overall

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:45.780
reduce the wastage of food.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:48.220
And little things like that
have been going on internally

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:49.982
for many, many, many years.

00:19:49.982 --> 00:19:51.940
And it's all of this
learning, all of this code

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:54.314
that we've been writing for
our own systems is what we've

00:19:54.314 --> 00:19:56.680
taken and brought to the
world as TensorFlow, which

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:00.110
is this machine learning
platform for everybody.

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:03.304
So as a programmer, the steps to
build a machine learning model

00:20:03.304 --> 00:20:05.470
are a little bit different
than what you're used to.

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:07.720
As a programmer, you might
get a design document

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:09.190
or a specification.

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:10.632
And then you decompose that.

00:20:10.632 --> 00:20:12.340
And then you write
some code around that.

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:15.072
And maybe you write some
throwaway prototyping code.

00:20:15.072 --> 00:20:16.780
And then that throwaway
prototyping code,

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:17.770
you'll learn
something from that.

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:18.561
And you keep going.

00:20:18.561 --> 00:20:19.540
You keep iterating.

00:20:19.540 --> 00:20:20.800
With machine learning,
it's different.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:22.690
But in some ways, it's very
similar from the iterating

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:23.290
part.

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:26.050
So the first thing that you need
to do is gather your data, OK?

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:27.850
You've got lots of
different data sources

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:29.180
that your data might be in.

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:30.520
There's lots of different
ways that your data

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:31.632
might be represented.

00:20:31.632 --> 00:20:32.590
Let me give an example.

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:34.900
Like in the breast cancer
that I've been talking about,

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:36.460
there's the size of the cell.

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:39.160
Say that size of the cell
might be a value from 10 to 30,

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:41.440
an integer or a long
value from 10 to 30.

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:42.940
That's probably not
very good for me

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:44.140
to train a neural network.

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:47.390
I might want to normalize that
data where the smallest cell is

00:20:47.390 --> 00:20:50.680
a 0 and the biggest one is a
1 so I've got a span of data.

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:52.800
And then I can be a little
bit more intelligent,

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.105
that I know the ones towards
the bigger end of the span

00:20:55.105 --> 00:20:56.980
are more likely to be
malignant, and the ones

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:59.170
towards the smaller end are
more likely to be benign--

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:01.420
things like that that you've
got to gather your data.

00:21:01.420 --> 00:21:03.520
And then you've got to ingest
that data into the programming

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:04.780
language that you're using.

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:07.840
Most people, when building
with TensorFlow, use Python.

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:09.790
But we've been expanding
TensorFlow recently.

00:21:09.790 --> 00:21:13.150
We've added Swift, and we've
added JavaScript recently.

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:15.310
And so think about
the data structures

00:21:15.310 --> 00:21:17.852
and the ways of
representing data that are

00:21:17.852 --> 00:21:19.060
available in those languages.

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:20.800
And you have to
ingest it into that.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:22.797
But even further
on, one extra step

00:21:22.797 --> 00:21:24.880
that you have to take as
a TensorFlow developer is

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:26.640
we've got something
called tf.data

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:29.140
where the idea is that, say,
for example, if you're a Python

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:32.500
developer and you are using
NumPy, that's great for you

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:36.290
to be able to train your models
off of, but it's not optimal.

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:37.630
It's not optimized.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:40.700
If you then take that and
convert that into tf.data,

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:42.870
which then convert
that into tensors--

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:45.010
and the idea of
TensorFlow is the concept

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:46.824
tensors are a
mathematical construct.

00:21:46.824 --> 00:21:48.490
And it's all about
flowing those tensors

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:50.860
through your neural network,
doing those calculations,

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:51.760
and being optimized.

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:54.670
So your first step, gathering
your data, you take it,

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:56.560
you stick it into your
programming language,

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:59.120
and then you optimize
that using tf.data.

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:02.200
And so then, the next part
is processing the data,

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:04.360
choosing your features,
and choosing your labels.

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.280
In the case of the breast cancer
that I've been speaking about,

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:07.720
there were 30 features.

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:10.270
I was able to train a
network that was just

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:13.630
as good at predicting a
malignant or a benign cell

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:17.154
using only six features as I
had from one that was using 30.

00:22:17.154 --> 00:22:18.820
And the one that used
those six features

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:21.050
took a fraction of
the time to train.

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:24.910
To give another example, there's
a common data set out there

00:22:24.910 --> 00:22:28.270
for house prices in California
that a lot of people

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:29.927
use for learning in
machine learning.

00:22:29.927 --> 00:22:32.260
And one thing I learned when
I was playing with that one

00:22:32.260 --> 00:22:34.210
that I thought was really
interesting was that, usually

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:35.960
when you're buying a
house, the number one

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:38.585
thing that dictates the price is
the number of bedrooms, right?

00:22:38.585 --> 00:22:40.793
A four-bedroom house costs
more than a three-bedroom,

00:22:40.793 --> 00:22:42.640
costs more than a
two-bedroom typically.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:43.750
But when training
a neural network

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:46.180
to actually predict house
prices, the number of bedrooms

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:47.680
had no impact.

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:50.240
I trained a model, and I got
a particular set of accuracy.

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:52.240
I trained it by removing
the number of bedrooms.

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:54.550
I got the same accuracy,
but it was quicker to train.

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:56.410
Because often, sometimes,
one piece of data

00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:57.790
is hidden in another.

00:22:57.790 --> 00:22:59.290
In this case, the
number of bedrooms

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:00.640
is hidden in the
size of the house.

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:02.806
A four-bedroom house is
bigger than a three-bedrooms

00:23:02.806 --> 00:23:04.120
is bigger than a two-bedroom.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:05.110
And it was the size
of the house that

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:06.880
was the bigger
signal in determining

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:08.120
the price of the house.

00:23:08.120 --> 00:23:10.480
So things like that is
processing data, formulating

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:11.804
it to optimize for ML--

00:23:11.804 --> 00:23:12.970
so a lot of trial and error.

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:15.517
And that's really what
we call "data science."

00:23:15.517 --> 00:23:17.350
Then of course, there's
training your model.

00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:19.180
That's where you're going to be
writing your TensorFlow code.

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:20.556
I'll show some in
a moment, where

00:23:20.556 --> 00:23:22.930
you're designing a network,
you're experimenting with it,

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:24.520
and you're optimizing
it for accuracy.

00:23:24.520 --> 00:23:26.519
And then finally, and
probably most importantly,

00:23:26.519 --> 00:23:27.980
is where do you use your model.

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:29.350
So there are a number
of different places

00:23:29.350 --> 00:23:29.940
you can use it.

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:31.090
There's a serving environment.

00:23:31.090 --> 00:23:33.130
We've got something called
TensorFlow Lite that will allow

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:34.213
you to run it on a mobile.

00:23:34.213 --> 00:23:35.600
I'll demo that in a moment.

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:37.840
And then it's a course of
continually optimizing,

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:40.294
improving, and
updating your code.

00:23:40.294 --> 00:23:41.710
So let's take a
quick look, first,

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:44.043
at gathering data, if we can
switch to the demo machine.

00:23:46.830 --> 00:23:49.120
Sorry, before we switch to
the demo machine-- sorry,

00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:51.260
go back to the
slides for a second.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:53.520
My screensaver's there,
my beautiful screensaver.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:55.270
Can we go back to the slides?

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:58.730
So a very famous
detective, Sherlock Holmes,

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:02.440
said "It's a capital mistake to
theorize before one has data."

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:05.540
And unfortunately, he's
fictional, but he's also right.

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:08.200
And so getting good data
and training your system

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.610
with good data is
very, very important.

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:12.850
So gathering data, it's
all about data science.

00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:15.550
Data is the lifeblood
of machine learning,

00:24:15.550 --> 00:24:17.620
I showed the diagram
earlier on where it's data

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:19.450
in, answers in, rules out.

00:24:19.450 --> 00:24:22.990
So you really-- the job
role of data scientist

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:25.240
has grown massively
in importance

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:27.860
in the last few years, and
this is the prime reason why.

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:30.850
Because you can write crap code
that will compile and still

00:24:30.850 --> 00:24:33.010
execute, right?

00:24:33.010 --> 00:24:35.620
A programmer-- the
really good programmer

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:37.830
is the one who writes
the really good code that

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:41.050
becomes easy to maintain, that
executes quickly, and doesn't

00:24:41.050 --> 00:24:42.530
have bugs in production.

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:44.040
It's the same kind
of thing in data.

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:45.070
It's gathering your data.

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:46.900
You can train a neural
network that's going to work,

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:48.775
but you could train it
on all the wrong data.

00:24:48.775 --> 00:24:51.620
And training could take a long
time and be very expensive.

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:53.200
The model may not be optimized.

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:56.460
So it ends up being, data is
the new code in many ways.

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:58.440
So one of the
things that you need

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:01.411
to help you get your data
ready, of course, is good tools.

00:25:01.411 --> 00:25:03.910
So one of the tools that we
have in Cloud that I like to use

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:05.044
is BigQuery.

00:25:05.044 --> 00:25:06.460
So the nice thing
with BigQuery is

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:08.543
it gives you this massive
data warehouse if you've

00:25:08.543 --> 00:25:09.627
got tons and tons of data.

00:25:09.627 --> 00:25:12.209
In my breast cancer example, I
only had a few hundred records.

00:25:12.209 --> 00:25:13.300
So I didn't need that.

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:15.610
But say you're training
off of millions of records.

00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:17.380
BigQuery is really
great for that.

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:19.060
Because not only
does it store it

00:25:19.060 --> 00:25:20.560
in a way that's
easily accessible

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:22.060
and it's quick
for you to access,

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:24.304
it also allows you to do
some analysis on your data

00:25:24.304 --> 00:25:25.720
and then download
it, for example,

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:27.640
as something like a
CSV that you can then

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:28.790
turn into a training model.

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:31.247
And right now, even though
CSV is quite primitive,

00:25:31.247 --> 00:25:32.830
it's the number one
data format that's

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:34.610
used in a lot of
machine learning.

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:36.505
So now can we switch
the demo machine please?

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:44.420
And just to give a quick look
at what BigQuery looks like,

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:45.650
I have a query running here.

00:25:45.650 --> 00:25:47.960
So there's a number
of public data sets

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:49.876
that we've put into BigQuery.

00:25:49.876 --> 00:25:51.500
And so these public
data sets-- and one

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:52.550
of the one that I've
been playing with

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:53.924
and I've been
having a lot of fun

00:25:53.924 --> 00:25:56.120
with is there's a
baseball data set.

00:25:56.120 --> 00:25:57.590
So I love to play
fantasy baseball.

00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:00.048
And I'm now giving away all of
my secrets of how I beat all

00:26:00.048 --> 00:26:01.460
my friends at fantasy baseball.

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:02.930
Because I've been
doing a lot of machine

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:04.346
learning stuff out
of the baseball

00:26:04.346 --> 00:26:06.320
data set that's in BigQuery.

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:08.190
And shh, don't tell them.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:10.754
And so then, the idea
is, like, BigQuery,

00:26:10.754 --> 00:26:12.170
this is a browser-based
interface.

00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:13.971
I'm doing it as a live demo now.

00:26:13.971 --> 00:26:16.220
So for example here, I'm
going to search for pitchers.

00:26:16.220 --> 00:26:18.860
And I'm going to search for my
third-favorite baseball player.

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:20.685
Can anybody guess who
it is from his name,

00:26:20.685 --> 00:26:23.770
for pitcher first
name equals Felix?

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:24.551
I'm a Seattle guy.

00:26:24.551 --> 00:26:25.550
That'll give you a clue.

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:26.642
But I run a query.

00:26:26.642 --> 00:26:28.850
And there you can say, it
just took like 1.2 seconds.

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:30.511
It's gone through
the entire data set.

00:26:30.511 --> 00:26:31.760
This is just a standard query.

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:32.634
It's a SQL query.

00:26:32.634 --> 00:26:34.550
But it's given me the
data from that data set.

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:36.069
And because it's
SQL, I can start

00:26:36.069 --> 00:26:38.360
doing interesting things like
that, like normalizing it

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:38.945
or whatever.

00:26:38.945 --> 00:26:40.820
Or I could just take
the data, and I can just

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:42.920
download it and then do
all the fun normalization

00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:45.470
stuff using tools,
or directly in Python

00:26:45.470 --> 00:26:49.370
or whatever language I'm using,
to put it into TensorFlow.

00:26:49.370 --> 00:26:52.920
So can we switch back
to the slides, please?

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:55.310
So the next step, then,
is processing your data.

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:57.980
And processing your data
is a little bit different

00:26:57.980 --> 00:27:02.870
than it would typically be
because of how you sometimes

00:27:02.870 --> 00:27:05.060
need to encode your
data to make it

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:06.377
understood by a neural network.

00:27:06.377 --> 00:27:08.960
So the first thing that you do,
as I've mentioned earlier, is,

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:11.150
whatever your
language-specific encoding is--

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:13.640
Python, JavaScript
arrays, Swift data,

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:15.060
all those kind of things--

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:18.710
take a look at this URL,
tensorflow.org/guide/datasets.

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:20.240
That will tell you
the optimal ways

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:22.340
to represent your data
in these languages

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:26.510
and then turn it into tf.data,
which is a format of data which

00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:28.670
is tensor-based, which
is then optimized

00:27:28.670 --> 00:27:29.960
for running in TensorFlow.

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:32.580
But more importantly is how
you think about your data.

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:35.170
And let me give one example
for your feature engineering.

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:37.484
And this is unintuitive
as a programmer.

00:27:37.484 --> 00:27:39.650
So say, take, for example,
I've got something that's

00:27:39.650 --> 00:27:41.300
got four different classes.

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:43.970
And my breast cancer was two,
right, malignant or benign.

00:27:43.970 --> 00:27:46.160
But say I want to classify
something that's got four

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:48.244
different possible classes.

00:27:48.244 --> 00:27:49.660
Now, as a programmer,
I'd say, OK,

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:51.740
if something has got
four possible options,

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:54.440
the optimal way for me to
store that is probably 2 bits,

00:27:54.440 --> 00:27:57.710
right-- you know
00, 01, 10, or 11.

00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:00.537
Or maybe slightly less optimal
is it's an integer, OK?

00:28:00.537 --> 00:28:01.620
I'll put it as an integer.

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:03.429
So it's going to
be 0, 1, 2 or 3.

00:28:03.429 --> 00:28:04.970
But in machine
learning, you actually

00:28:04.970 --> 00:28:07.670
use four integers to store it.

00:28:07.670 --> 00:28:10.310
And you go, this seems crazy.

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:13.250
Why would I use four integers
to store a value that

00:28:13.250 --> 00:28:15.590
could be between 0 and 3?

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:19.880
So the reason for this is that--
start thinking in terms of,

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:22.062
we want to run
classification on something.

00:28:22.062 --> 00:28:23.520
Remember, we're
giving it the data.

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:24.540
We're giving it the answers.

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:27.160
And it's inferring in the rules
and giving us something out.

00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:29.050
So in this case,
I would say, OK,

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:31.020
there's going to be
four neurons at the end

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:32.167
of the neural network.

00:28:32.167 --> 00:28:34.500
And these four neurons are
going to give me a percentage

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:35.916
probability that
it is what it is.

00:28:35.916 --> 00:28:39.360
Like, I don't know, pick
something that there's four of.

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:42.565
I don't know-- soccer teams in
a group in the World Cup, right?

00:28:42.565 --> 00:28:43.190
So what was it?

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:46.690
It was England, Belgium,
Tunisia, and Saudi Arabia,

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:47.840
I think, were in one group.

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:49.590
So imagine if I was
building a probability

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:52.620
that these ones are going to
win that group in the World Cup.

00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:56.142
Now instead of outputting a 0
for England, a 1 for Belgium,

00:28:56.142 --> 00:28:57.600
that kind of thing,
it's a case of,

00:28:57.600 --> 00:28:59.660
I have four neurons
that are going to fire.

00:28:59.660 --> 00:29:01.260
And I want this
neuron to fire if I'm

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:02.440
predicting England to win.

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:05.250
I want this neuron to fire if
I'm predicting Belgium to win,

00:29:05.250 --> 00:29:06.580
that type of thing.

00:29:06.580 --> 00:29:08.250
And as a result, I
have to pass the data

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:10.170
into it that looks like that.

00:29:10.170 --> 00:29:12.810
So when I pass the data in
for, these are the players,

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:15.570
this is the form,
this is how they

00:29:15.570 --> 00:29:17.850
play in a certain temperature,
on a certain field, all

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:20.100
of those kind of data,
when I pass in the answer,

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:22.750
then I also want the
answer to be 1, 0,0,

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:25.986
0 when it's England
were good, 0, 1, 0,

00:29:25.986 --> 00:29:28.110
0 when it's Belgium are
good, those kind of things.

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:30.690
So that's the pattern for
the neuron that I want.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:33.730
So this process-- and
this is just one concept.

00:29:33.730 --> 00:29:35.460
This is called One-Hot encoding.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:38.220
But this is part of the
process of feature engineering.

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:40.110
So as a software
developer in TensorFlow,

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:42.426
these are the type
of terms and these

00:29:42.426 --> 00:29:43.800
are the type of
concepts that you

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:47.100
need to learn so that
you can design your data,

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.960
you can engineer the
features of your data

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.732
so that you can train
neural networks around it.

00:29:51.732 --> 00:29:53.940
So I want to give a quick
look at feature engineering

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:55.050
in Python.

00:29:55.050 --> 00:29:58.570
So we can switch back
to the demo machine.

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:00.550
So the best thing to
do, I would say, is--

00:30:00.550 --> 00:30:03.800
if you go to
tensorflow.org/tutorials,

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:06.850
this website teaches you how
to get started with TensorFlow.

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:10.360
And if I scroll down
here, these five links--

00:30:10.360 --> 00:30:12.820
basic classification,
text classification,

00:30:12.820 --> 00:30:14.980
regression, overfitting,
underfitting, and saving

00:30:14.980 --> 00:30:15.850
and loading--

00:30:15.850 --> 00:30:18.900
these will lead you to something
called a Colab notebook.

00:30:18.900 --> 00:30:21.070
And the Colab notebook
looks like this.

00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:23.170
And what's really neat
about the Colab notebook

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:25.128
is that it's something
that executes completely

00:30:25.128 --> 00:30:28.420
in the browser, but it gives
you the full TensorFlow runtime.

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:31.660
And it gives you the full
TensorFlow code and API.

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:33.370
And you can even run
it on a GPU if you

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:35.912
want to do training on a GPU,
which is more optimized for it.

00:30:35.912 --> 00:30:37.828
And this is something
that runs in the browser

00:30:37.828 --> 00:30:38.830
and is completely free.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:41.414
And what we've done is we've
built these number of tutorials

00:30:41.414 --> 00:30:43.330
that will say-- for
example, in this tutorial,

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:45.700
it's like, if I want to
do a binary classification

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:48.640
around movie reviews, it
has all the code in there.

00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:50.620
And it allows you to
step through that code.

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:53.020
And it will do things such
as the feature engineering

00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:53.860
that I've mentioned.

00:30:53.860 --> 00:30:55.390
Here it's preparing the data.

00:30:55.390 --> 00:30:56.900
Here it's building the model.

00:30:56.900 --> 00:31:00.440
Here it's putting in the loss
function, those kind of things.

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:03.460
And this will take you through
all the steps of, like, here's

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:05.425
a raw feed of data from IMDb.

00:31:05.425 --> 00:31:08.050
And what we're going to do with
this raw feed of data from IMDb

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:09.841
is we're going to build
this classification

00:31:09.841 --> 00:31:11.775
of good movie versus bad movie.

00:31:11.775 --> 00:31:13.900
And it'll take you through
the steps of doing that.

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:16.075
So time is a little short, so
I won't go through those steps

00:31:16.075 --> 00:31:16.575
now.

00:31:16.575 --> 00:31:17.880
But all of this is public.

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:20.026
And I'd strongly recommend
to go take a look at it

00:31:20.026 --> 00:31:21.400
and step through
these notebooks.

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:22.850
It doesn't take long.

00:31:22.850 --> 00:31:26.500
And you'll find them all on
tensorflow.org/tutorials.

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:30.380
OK, if we could switch
back to the slides, please.

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:32.416
So the next step--
remember gathering

00:31:32.416 --> 00:31:34.040
your data was the
first step, repairing

00:31:34.040 --> 00:31:35.440
your data was the second.

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:37.117
The next step, and
the most important

00:31:37.117 --> 00:31:39.700
from a programmatic step-- from
a programmatic point of view--

00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:41.127
is training your models.

00:31:41.127 --> 00:31:43.210
So first of all, when
you're training your models,

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:45.874
there's three
different types of chip

00:31:45.874 --> 00:31:47.290
that you can use
in your computer.

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.420
We're all familiar with the CPU.

00:31:49.420 --> 00:31:51.860
And a CPU, to me, is
like a Swiss army knife.

00:31:51.860 --> 00:31:54.160
It's a chip that's been
designed to handle everything

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:56.110
in your computer,
managing your windows,

00:31:56.110 --> 00:31:58.900
managing your network stack,
all of this kind of stuff.

00:31:58.900 --> 00:32:01.030
Your code compiles and
it executes on that.

00:32:01.030 --> 00:32:02.890
So as a result, it's
a general purpose chip

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:04.640
for doing just about anything.

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:07.780
So therefore, it's not optimized
for any one particular thing.

00:32:07.780 --> 00:32:09.850
It's just like a
Swiss army knife.

00:32:09.850 --> 00:32:12.040
The second type
of chip is a GPU.

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:13.940
And a GPU is kind
of interesting,

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:15.830
because it's a graphics
processing unit.

00:32:15.830 --> 00:32:17.455
And a graphics
processing unit, they've

00:32:17.455 --> 00:32:20.380
evolved greatly for
games over recent years.

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:23.604
But the things that you do in
a game such as 3D rotation,

00:32:23.604 --> 00:32:26.020
the matrix multiplication for
that, all that kind of stuff

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:27.610
is actually very
similar to the math

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:29.194
that you would do
in a neural network.

00:32:29.194 --> 00:32:31.401
If you remember earlier, I
was talking about the math

00:32:31.401 --> 00:32:32.695
and calculating the weights.

00:32:32.695 --> 00:32:34.570
It's very, very similar
to the kind of things

00:32:34.570 --> 00:32:35.920
that are done in a GPU.

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:37.810
And of course, a GPU is
a type of chip that's

00:32:37.810 --> 00:32:39.370
got lots of little cores.

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:42.450
And all of these little cores
are optimized for doing math.

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:44.110
And as a result,
for a long time,

00:32:44.110 --> 00:32:45.880
the researchers who
have been trying

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:48.400
to optimize what they're
getting out of machine learning

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:49.960
have been using GPUs.

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:51.850
And it's a chip that
works really great,

00:32:51.850 --> 00:32:53.470
but it's not designed
for the task,

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:56.330
like slicing a chili
pepper with a razor blade.

00:32:56.330 --> 00:32:58.750
And so then, the
third one is a TPU.

00:32:58.750 --> 00:33:01.780
And the TPU is a chip that we've
built and we've made available.

00:33:01.780 --> 00:33:03.890
It's called a Tensor
Processing Unit--

00:33:03.890 --> 00:33:05.140
so T for Tensor--

00:33:05.140 --> 00:33:07.450
with the idea being
that this is absolutely

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:09.580
optimized for machine learning.

00:33:09.580 --> 00:33:11.820
It's optimized for
processing tensors.

00:33:11.820 --> 00:33:14.320
And as a result, it's like a
specialized knife, like a sushi

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:16.770
knife, in this case.

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:19.540
So this is a TPU and some
of the numbers behind it.

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:22.190
We get about 180
teraflops per device.

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:24.020
Bandwidth memory-- 64 gig.

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:25.997
The reason for that
high-bandwidth memory

00:33:25.997 --> 00:33:28.580
is when you think about loading
data, processing data, loading

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:29.220
data.

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:31.656
So it really improves
your data throughput.

00:33:31.656 --> 00:33:34.280
It's programmed via TensorFlow,
and it's now available in beta.

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:36.187
So if you use the
Google Cloud Platform,

00:33:36.187 --> 00:33:38.270
you can get a machine in
the Google Cloud Platform

00:33:38.270 --> 00:33:39.230
that uses a TPU.

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:42.460
And then you can just
rent time on that TPU

00:33:42.460 --> 00:33:44.070
to do your training.

00:33:44.070 --> 00:33:46.400
And one example that
we've done was--

00:33:46.400 --> 00:33:50.300
there's an image classification
thing called ResNet where,

00:33:50.300 --> 00:33:53.120
as you can see here, we
did a throughput of taking

00:33:53.120 --> 00:33:54.910
real images to train in ResNet.

00:33:54.910 --> 00:33:58.850
We were able to, with TensorFlow
1.7, about 2,500 images

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:00.740
per second with a TPU.

00:34:00.740 --> 00:34:03.050
But then when we
preprocessed those images

00:34:03.050 --> 00:34:04.760
to make them a little
bit more optimal,

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:09.350
that went up to 3,150 per second
being able to process on a TPU.

00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:10.850
But the important
bullet point here

00:34:10.850 --> 00:34:13.400
is the third one,
that the ResNet--

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:16.480
for many years, researchers
have been working on this.

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:18.080
And to train a
model in ResNet used

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:19.914
to cost in the tens of
thousands of dollars,

00:34:19.914 --> 00:34:21.455
and then in the
thousands of dollars,

00:34:21.455 --> 00:34:23.010
and then in the
hundreds of dollars.

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:25.493
And we did this one
benchmark back in March

00:34:25.493 --> 00:34:26.659
where we actually tested it.

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:29.221
It was about $85 in CPU time.

00:34:29.221 --> 00:34:30.929
And I was actually
working on this slide.

00:34:30.929 --> 00:34:33.110
It was used in our keynote
at the TensorFlow Summit

00:34:33.110 --> 00:34:34.138
that Jeff Dean gave.

00:34:34.138 --> 00:34:36.679
And as we were working on the
slide, they came back and said,

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:38.820
they've actually
slashed it even further,

00:34:38.820 --> 00:34:40.947
but they haven't verified
by how much exactly.

00:34:40.947 --> 00:34:42.530
But I'm not allowed
to say the number,

00:34:42.530 --> 00:34:44.570
but it's significantly
lower than this.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:46.070
So we have a kind
of a Moore's law

00:34:46.070 --> 00:34:48.409
in effect for the actual
cost for training,

00:34:48.409 --> 00:34:50.790
where it's plummeting
dramatically.

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:54.590
And using TPUs in particular,
even though a TPU might be,

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:57.287
on per second basis, more
expensive than a GPU,

00:34:57.287 --> 00:34:58.870
when you can do that
much more with it

00:34:58.870 --> 00:35:00.380
and train the model
much cheaper--

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:02.390
and if you go to
this URL at Stanford,

00:35:02.390 --> 00:35:04.430
you will see our first
benchmark submission

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:06.530
that we put around it.

00:35:06.530 --> 00:35:08.670
So then the next part is
actually using the models.

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:10.270
So you've gathered your data.

00:35:10.270 --> 00:35:11.540
You've processed your data.

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:12.623
You've trained your model.

00:35:12.623 --> 00:35:13.710
How do you use them?

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:17.300
And so I'm very passionate
about mobile and using mobile.

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:19.190
But there is three
different things--

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:21.500
three different ways that
you can use machine learning

00:35:21.500 --> 00:35:22.130
models.

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:24.088
So first of all is what
I've been talking about

00:35:24.088 --> 00:35:24.707
is TensorFlow.

00:35:24.707 --> 00:35:27.290
TensorFlow itself, it's designed
so that you can train models.

00:35:27.290 --> 00:35:28.950
There's an API for
you to train models.

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:31.500
And it's a runtime for you to
execute them on your machine.

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:33.333
But you know what, if
you've trained a model

00:35:33.333 --> 00:35:35.710
and you've got a nice, big,
fat GPU under your desk,

00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:38.001
and you've trained a model
on that, and you can execute

00:35:38.001 --> 00:35:39.851
and you can infer
on that, so what?

00:35:39.851 --> 00:35:41.600
Not anybody else can
use the model, right?

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:42.975
It's great if
you're an academic,

00:35:42.975 --> 00:35:44.474
and you're researching,
and you want

00:35:44.474 --> 00:35:47.120
to show that, hey, I used this
machine, and I have this model,

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:48.121
and here's my benchmark.

00:35:48.121 --> 00:35:50.203
But it's not very good for
you to deploy the model

00:35:50.203 --> 00:35:51.230
for other people to use.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.930
So we've been hard at work
to build two runtimes.

00:35:53.930 --> 00:35:55.986
The first of these
is TensorFlow Lite,

00:35:55.986 --> 00:35:57.860
where the idea is that
if you have an Android

00:35:57.860 --> 00:35:59.660
device or an iOS device--

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:02.130
in an increasing
amount of IoT devices,

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:04.779
you've probably seen, we've
put demos out for Raspberry Pi,

00:36:04.779 --> 00:36:07.070
where the idea is, if you've
got a Raspberry Pi device,

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:09.260
you can then run inference
on those devices.

00:36:09.260 --> 00:36:12.200
And by that I mean you build
the model on your big, fat GPU

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:14.357
under your desk, or
you build it on a TPU.

00:36:14.357 --> 00:36:16.190
And then you can actually
crunch that model,

00:36:16.190 --> 00:36:17.960
flatten it, prepare
it for mobile,

00:36:17.960 --> 00:36:19.862
and then deploy it
to a mobile device.

00:36:19.862 --> 00:36:21.320
And then on that
mobile device, you

00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:23.390
do not need any
connection to the internet

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:25.340
to be able to run
inference on the model.

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:28.170
I have a video that I'll
show later if we have time.

00:36:28.170 --> 00:36:31.566
But it's a terrific example
of, there's a crop in Africa--

00:36:31.566 --> 00:36:32.690
well, it's a crop all over.

00:36:32.690 --> 00:36:35.300
But it's used-- it's
part of the food group

00:36:35.300 --> 00:36:37.070
in Africa called cassava.

00:36:37.070 --> 00:36:39.500
And we worked with a group
in the country of Tanzania,

00:36:39.500 --> 00:36:43.500
where cassava was primarily
the backup plant for farmers.

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:45.810
They had their primary plants
that they wanted to grow,

00:36:45.810 --> 00:36:48.352
but they had cassava if
their primary plant failed.

00:36:48.352 --> 00:36:50.810
So if the primary plant failed,
you wouldn't have a famine.

00:36:50.810 --> 00:36:53.342
But if the cassava
failed, it's a disaster.

00:36:53.342 --> 00:36:55.550
Now the thing about cassava
that's really interesting

00:36:55.550 --> 00:36:57.860
is that there is
a disease, there's

00:36:57.860 --> 00:36:59.720
a blight that affects cassava.

00:36:59.720 --> 00:37:03.962
But you don't see this with the
human eye until it is too late.

00:37:03.962 --> 00:37:05.420
You'll see splotches
on the leaves.

00:37:05.420 --> 00:37:06.860
It's too late by that point.

00:37:06.860 --> 00:37:07.970
The plant is blighted.

00:37:07.970 --> 00:37:09.886
And the blight has spread
to the other plants.

00:37:09.886 --> 00:37:11.010
You just don't see it yet.

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:12.926
So as a result, a blight
coming in the cassava

00:37:12.926 --> 00:37:14.570
can actually destroy
an entire crop

00:37:14.570 --> 00:37:16.379
before you know it's
being destroyed.

00:37:16.379 --> 00:37:18.920
So we worked with a university,
and we worked with some folks

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:22.620
in Tanzania, to build
a detector to say--

00:37:22.620 --> 00:37:25.170
to train it on leaves and
say, OK, this is a leaf.

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:26.790
We don't know if
it's diseased or not.

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:27.830
But in the future,
we'll label it

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:29.727
if that plant becomes
diseased, and train it

00:37:29.727 --> 00:37:30.560
on a bunch of these.

00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:32.270
And it ended up
being able to spot,

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:34.330
on a disconnected device--
most importantly, it's

00:37:34.330 --> 00:37:37.160
a disconnected device, so
no broadband connectivity--

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:39.110
that farmers can take
an old Android device,

00:37:39.110 --> 00:37:41.720
wave it over a
leaf, and determine,

00:37:41.720 --> 00:37:43.700
much earlier than you
could with the human eye,

00:37:43.700 --> 00:37:45.177
if the leaf is diseased or not.

00:37:45.177 --> 00:37:47.510
And if it is diseased, then
they can yank that plant out

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:49.370
before it destroys
the rest of the crop.

00:37:49.370 --> 00:37:51.560
So little scenarios like
that have been empowered

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:53.030
using this kind of thing.

00:37:53.030 --> 00:37:53.840
Train it.

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:54.690
You're labeling.

00:37:54.690 --> 00:37:55.930
You're giving the data.

00:37:55.930 --> 00:37:56.930
You're creating a model.

00:37:56.930 --> 00:37:58.490
You're flattening that model.

00:37:58.490 --> 00:37:59.990
And then, using
TensorFlow Lite, you

00:37:59.990 --> 00:38:04.130
can then run it on an Android
device or an iOS device.

00:38:04.130 --> 00:38:06.266
And then the third of
these is TensorFlow.js.

00:38:06.266 --> 00:38:08.390
And this one, to me, is
also particularly exciting.

00:38:08.390 --> 00:38:11.480
Because now you can use
JavaScript in the browser,

00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:14.180
not just to run inference
on existing models,

00:38:14.180 --> 00:38:15.920
but to actually
build the models.

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:17.810
So all those steps that
I was talking about

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:19.430
of gathering your data,
preparing your data,

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:21.170
training the model,
and running the model

00:38:21.170 --> 00:38:23.220
can be done directly
within a browser.

00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:24.890
So think about the
distributed scenarios

00:38:24.890 --> 00:38:26.150
that are available there.

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:28.382
If you go to
youtube.com/tensorflow,

00:38:28.382 --> 00:38:29.840
we've got a lot of
videos on there.

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:32.256
And I'm actually in the middle
of producing a video series

00:38:32.256 --> 00:38:35.120
at the moment on doing
this in JavaScript.

00:38:35.120 --> 00:38:36.650
So if you're a
JavaScript developer,

00:38:36.650 --> 00:38:38.327
the basics of getting
up and running

00:38:38.327 --> 00:38:40.160
with creating a neural
network, training it,

00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:42.160
and doing inference in
the browser are on there.

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:43.852
So go take a look at them.

00:38:43.852 --> 00:38:45.560
So I'm going to switch
back to TensorFlow

00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:47.601
Lite for a second, which
is the mobile one that I

00:38:47.601 --> 00:38:48.450
was talking about.

00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:50.180
So the idea of how
that works is you've

00:38:50.180 --> 00:38:53.390
built your model using your
big, fat GPU or your TPU.

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:56.150
And then we've created
a converter for that.

00:38:56.150 --> 00:38:58.790
So the process that
I called flattening,

00:38:58.790 --> 00:39:00.730
that converts it to
TensorFlow Lite format.

00:39:00.730 --> 00:39:02.480
And then there's an
interpreter that we've

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:04.630
built for iOS, another
one for Android,

00:39:04.630 --> 00:39:07.310
another one for IoT
devices, that will then

00:39:07.310 --> 00:39:09.750
execute that model for you.

00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:12.311
And depending on the hardware
that you're using, then

00:39:12.311 --> 00:39:14.060
there are hardware
acceleration delegates.

00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:16.732
So more recent Android
phones, for example,

00:39:16.732 --> 00:39:19.190
are able to use some of the
hardware acceleration delegates

00:39:19.190 --> 00:39:21.380
that are on there, things
such as the Pixel 2,

00:39:21.380 --> 00:39:24.460
so you can have even
faster inference on those.

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:26.210
We've also tried to
make it cross-platform

00:39:26.210 --> 00:39:27.320
as much as we could.

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:31.730
So that converted to TF Lite
format will convert it so it

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:35.060
can be run on an Android app
using Java, Kotlin, or C++,

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:38.090
on an iOS app, only
using C++ at the moment--

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:40.490
we haven't yet built the
extensions to allow Objective-C

00:39:40.490 --> 00:39:41.810
or Swift to be used--

00:39:41.810 --> 00:39:44.300
and then also on Linux and
some other IoT devices.

00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:45.970
And there's some
ongoing stuff on there

00:39:45.970 --> 00:39:47.678
and some announcements
coming pretty soon

00:39:47.678 --> 00:39:49.370
about that to watch out for.

00:39:49.370 --> 00:39:51.687
There's some really exciting
stuff happening in IoT.

00:39:51.687 --> 00:39:53.270
And if you're an iOS
developer and you

00:39:53.270 --> 00:39:55.810
want to use CoreML, then of
course you can use that too.

00:39:55.810 --> 00:39:58.430
We've created a converter
to CoreML format.

00:39:58.430 --> 00:40:01.250
So you can go off and use
Swift or Objective-C on CoreML

00:40:01.250 --> 00:40:04.052
once you've built your model
and been able to convert it.

00:40:04.052 --> 00:40:06.260
One of the neat things that
we always get asked about

00:40:06.260 --> 00:40:06.920
is the size.

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:10.310
What impact is this going to
have on my Android device?

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:12.020
Well, we found that
the core interpreter

00:40:12.020 --> 00:40:15.830
to interpret a model
is about 75 kilobytes.

00:40:15.830 --> 00:40:19.430
Now TensorFlow Lite is in the
very early stages right now.

00:40:19.430 --> 00:40:22.060
So in the early stages, what
we've done is we've built it,

00:40:22.060 --> 00:40:24.110
and we've optimized it
for a number of scenarios.

00:40:24.110 --> 00:40:26.151
And the first scenario
that we've been working on

00:40:26.151 --> 00:40:27.610
is image classification.

00:40:27.610 --> 00:40:29.560
So a lot of the common
image classification

00:40:29.560 --> 00:40:31.740
models, all of the
operations that they use

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:33.040
are currently supported.

00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:34.900
So if you train something,
like for example,

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:36.760
with MobileNet or
ResNet, and then you

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:38.746
convert that to TensorFlow
Lite, it will work.

00:40:38.746 --> 00:40:40.870
But if you train something
custom that isn't those,

00:40:40.870 --> 00:40:43.300
like for example, my
breast cancer classifier,

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:46.000
then that neural network
might use some operations that

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:47.510
are not supported.

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:51.430
And so we're continuing to work
on having operational support

00:40:51.430 --> 00:40:53.664
parity between TensorFlow
and TensorFlow Lite.

00:40:53.664 --> 00:40:55.830
But right now, with all
supported apps on TensorFlow

00:40:55.830 --> 00:40:58.650
Lite, it's about 400 kilobytes.

00:40:58.650 --> 00:41:02.570
OK, let me give a quick demo
of image recognition on mobile.

00:41:02.570 --> 00:41:04.960
So this will be
image recognition,

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:07.640
if we can switch to
the demo machine.

00:41:07.640 --> 00:41:12.760
And I'm going to run this on
an Android emulator, which

00:41:12.760 --> 00:41:15.760
is a little bit risky.

00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:18.430
So I'm just running my
emulator here right now.

00:41:18.430 --> 00:41:19.930
And what this is
doing is that this

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:23.860
is capturing from the camera on
the front of my laptop, which

00:41:23.860 --> 00:41:26.680
is emulated as the camera on
the front of an Android device.

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:27.906
And it's taking each frame.

00:41:27.906 --> 00:41:29.530
And it's passing that
through a network

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:32.920
that's been trained to classify
1,000 different images.

00:41:32.920 --> 00:41:34.480
So the output from
that network is

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:37.510
going to be 1,000 different
numbers, with each number being

00:41:37.510 --> 00:41:39.850
a probability that
what you're looking at

00:41:39.850 --> 00:41:42.340
is the thing represented
by that number.

00:41:42.340 --> 00:41:44.860
Like, I think, for example,
number 55 is a coffee

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:46.580
cup, those type of things.

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:49.070
So of those 1,000 now, it's
looking at the dots behind me

00:41:49.070 --> 00:41:49.570
here.

00:41:49.570 --> 00:41:52.690
And it's classifying that as
a shower curtain, or a window

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:55.320
screen, or a strainer,
which is pretty good.

00:41:55.320 --> 00:41:58.150
But I'm going to
do my banana demo.

00:41:58.150 --> 00:41:59.887
So I'm just going
to hold this up,

00:41:59.887 --> 00:42:01.720
because it sometimes
gets a little confused.

00:42:01.720 --> 00:42:04.779
And if I hold this
in front of it--

00:42:04.779 --> 00:42:06.070
can never know the orientation.

00:42:06.070 --> 00:42:06.570
There we go.

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:10.330
And you see it's actually--
well, it was a banana.

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:12.060
There we go, 76%
chance it's a banana.

00:42:12.060 --> 00:42:12.970
Oh, syringe, wow.

00:42:16.556 --> 00:42:18.430
I don't know why it
thought that's a syringe.

00:42:18.430 --> 00:42:20.010
My favorite is, I usually
stand in front of it,

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:21.740
and it gives me
something different.

00:42:21.740 --> 00:42:23.280
I was an Italian
greyhound earlier.

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:24.145
I don't know why.

00:42:24.145 --> 00:42:25.011
It must be my hair.

00:42:25.011 --> 00:42:25.510
There we go.

00:42:28.156 --> 00:42:29.060
I'm a strainer.

00:42:31.930 --> 00:42:34.359
But thinking about
what's going on there,

00:42:34.359 --> 00:42:35.650
there's 1,000 different things.

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:38.140
Remember, earlier, I was talking
about cats versus dogs, right?

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:39.040
That was two different things.

00:42:39.040 --> 00:42:40.870
But here, there's
1,000 different things.

00:42:40.870 --> 00:42:43.300
The model for this is just
a few hundred kilobytes.

00:42:43.300 --> 00:42:44.950
It's running that
because it's been

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:47.200
flattened to run on Android.

00:42:47.200 --> 00:42:49.300
And it's just a simple
Android application

00:42:49.300 --> 00:42:51.310
that's reading a
frame from the camera,

00:42:51.310 --> 00:42:54.310
passing that frame to the
model, getting the array back

00:42:54.310 --> 00:42:57.719
from the model of the 1,000
things, picking the top three,

00:42:57.719 --> 00:42:59.260
figuring out why
those top three are,

00:42:59.260 --> 00:43:00.734
and then showing
it on the screen.

00:43:00.734 --> 00:43:02.650
And then that number is
the time of inference.

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:06.050
So you can see it's about
1,300, 1,400 milliseconds.

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:08.350
But that's because it's
running in the emulator, OK?

00:43:08.350 --> 00:43:10.120
I have my Pixel 2 phone with me.

00:43:10.120 --> 00:43:12.200
If you want to see a demo
of it later on a phone,

00:43:12.200 --> 00:43:14.200
you'll actually see it
doing this classification

00:43:14.200 --> 00:43:16.360
in, usually, 30 or
40 microseconds.

00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:18.476
It's that fast at
being able to do it.

00:43:18.476 --> 00:43:20.350
The code for this is
completely open-sourced.

00:43:20.350 --> 00:43:21.640
It's on tensorflow.org.

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:22.210
Go have fun.

00:43:22.210 --> 00:43:23.350
Have a play with it.

00:43:23.350 --> 00:43:25.050
Also, there's some
codelabs outside.

00:43:25.050 --> 00:43:27.359
There's one codelab called
TensorFlow for Poets, which

00:43:27.359 --> 00:43:28.900
will teach you all
about this and how

00:43:28.900 --> 00:43:31.600
you can retrain this to
classify your own things.

00:43:31.600 --> 00:43:34.090
Like maybe you want to classify
different types of flower,

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:36.760
or something along those lines,
instead of the 1,000 classes

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:37.570
that it gives you.

00:43:37.570 --> 00:43:39.260
And it will show
you how to do that.

00:43:39.260 --> 00:43:41.320
So if we can switch
back to my slides.

00:43:44.140 --> 00:43:48.760
OK, so TensorFlow.js
is the next one.

00:43:48.760 --> 00:43:51.580
And so if we think
about TensorFlow.js,

00:43:51.580 --> 00:43:53.690
the idea with TensorFlow.js,
as I mentioned,

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:56.182
is that you can train a neural
network using JavaScript.

00:43:56.182 --> 00:43:58.390
And you can then run inference
on that neural network

00:43:58.390 --> 00:43:59.286
using JavaScript.

00:43:59.286 --> 00:44:00.910
Now, I've been talking
about JavaScript

00:44:00.910 --> 00:44:02.354
in the browser as one scenario.

00:44:02.354 --> 00:44:05.020
But there's a second scenario if
you're a JavaScript programmer,

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:07.330
and that's JavaScript
on Node.js.

00:44:07.330 --> 00:44:09.670
So if you have a Node.js
server, that server

00:44:09.670 --> 00:44:11.610
can be used to run
inference for you.

00:44:11.610 --> 00:44:14.109
Or that server can be used to
gather data and train things.

00:44:14.109 --> 00:44:16.150
And where that, to me,
gets particularly exciting

00:44:16.150 --> 00:44:18.760
is-- any Firebase fans here?

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:19.260
A few, cool.

00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:21.343
So you are probably familiar
with something called

00:44:21.343 --> 00:44:22.642
Cloud Functions for Firebase.

00:44:22.642 --> 00:44:24.850
And the nice thing about
Cloud Functions for Firebase

00:44:24.850 --> 00:44:27.100
is that they are a
serverless environment

00:44:27.100 --> 00:44:28.840
that you can write code on.

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:32.006
And then that code will execute
in response to triggers.

00:44:32.006 --> 00:44:33.880
So there can be triggers
in your application,

00:44:33.880 --> 00:44:36.910
like maybe a user logging
in or a particular analytics

00:44:36.910 --> 00:44:37.930
event happening.

00:44:37.930 --> 00:44:39.460
And then code could execute.

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:41.950
But now, instead of traditional
JavaScript if-then code

00:44:41.950 --> 00:44:44.732
executing, that JavaScript
could wrap a model.

00:44:44.732 --> 00:44:46.690
And then you could run
inference on that model,

00:44:46.690 --> 00:44:49.610
maybe to do a classification
or something along those lines.

00:44:49.610 --> 00:44:51.110
So that's TensorFlow.js.

00:44:51.110 --> 00:44:53.350
And if I take, a little bit,
a look at the code again

00:44:53.350 --> 00:44:55.960
before I show a demo, just
remember the ML paradigm.

00:44:55.960 --> 00:44:59.830
It's all about answers in,
data in, and rules out.

00:44:59.830 --> 00:45:02.680
If you take nothing else away
from this, as a programmer,

00:45:02.680 --> 00:45:04.387
start thinking in those terms.

00:45:04.387 --> 00:45:05.845
Start thinking
about what scenarios

00:45:05.845 --> 00:45:09.010
am I going to get if I put
answers in, and I put data in,

00:45:09.010 --> 00:45:10.400
and I get rules out.

00:45:10.400 --> 00:45:13.000
And I'm going to give
one final demo of that.

00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:14.630
And this one is a tough demo.

00:45:14.630 --> 00:45:16.810
So let's see if we can
switch to the demo machine.

00:45:16.810 --> 00:45:19.416
All right, so this
is a public demo.

00:45:19.416 --> 00:45:21.040
If you want to try
it out for yourself,

00:45:21.040 --> 00:45:23.230
it's on js.tensorflow.org.

00:45:23.230 --> 00:45:25.590
And this is "Pac-Man."

00:45:25.590 --> 00:45:28.281
So if you're like me, if
you like Google Doodles,

00:45:28.281 --> 00:45:30.780
if you remember, there was a
Google Doodle where we actually

00:45:30.780 --> 00:45:33.540
created a "Pac-Man" maze
out of the Google logo.

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:36.750
So what this demo does is, in
the browser, you can see here,

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:39.210
there are four boxes
corresponding to up, down,

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:40.500
left, and right.

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:43.950
So what I can do is I can start
capturing images of me maybe

00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:46.409
pointing up, or looking up, or
something along those lines.

00:45:46.409 --> 00:45:48.366
And I'm actually going
to start doing that now.

00:45:48.366 --> 00:45:49.440
I'll do it by pointing.

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:52.760
So I'm going to start capturing
some images of pointing up.

00:45:52.760 --> 00:45:54.220
I'll move it around
a little bit,

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:56.420
because they all look
a little bit different.

00:45:56.420 --> 00:45:57.820
I don't know, having
the dotted background,

00:45:57.820 --> 00:45:59.236
if that's going
to ruin it or not.

00:45:59.236 --> 00:46:00.600
We'll see-- so about 80 of them.

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:02.350
And I'm going to capture
some images of me

00:46:02.350 --> 00:46:07.072
pointing right, la, la, la, la,
get about 80 of them as well.

00:46:10.310 --> 00:46:11.630
Down is always the hard one.

00:46:11.630 --> 00:46:14.340
I'm going to catch some
images of me pointing down,

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:15.831
bleh, bleh, bleh, bleh.

00:46:15.831 --> 00:46:18.080
I can't see how many of them,
because it scrolled off.

00:46:18.080 --> 00:46:20.490
I'll just guess it's about 80.

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:22.030
OK, and I'm going
to capture images

00:46:22.030 --> 00:46:24.113
of me pointing to the left,
try to get my head out

00:46:24.113 --> 00:46:27.330
of the frame, bleh,
bleh, bleh, bleh.

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:29.580
And remember, this is all
just running in the browser.

00:46:29.580 --> 00:46:31.520
I'm using the webcam
on my machine.

00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:32.800
Capture a bunch of them.

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:35.030
OK, so now I'm going
to train the model.

00:46:35.030 --> 00:46:37.760
So when I train the model,
after I press the button,

00:46:37.760 --> 00:46:40.200
you'll see a number
underneath, which is the loss.

00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:41.720
So what that's doing is--
if you remember earlier,

00:46:41.720 --> 00:46:44.095
when I was showing-- it's
calculating something, figuring

00:46:44.095 --> 00:46:45.920
it out, comparing
it with the results,

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:49.040
seeing how well it did,
seeing it did really badly,

00:46:49.040 --> 00:46:50.837
try something else,
and keep going.

00:46:50.837 --> 00:46:51.920
And so it will keep going.

00:46:51.920 --> 00:46:54.630
And you'll see, the loss
should keep reducing.

00:46:54.630 --> 00:46:55.994
So there, now the loss is bad.

00:46:55.994 --> 00:46:57.160
It's going down, down, down.

00:46:57.160 --> 00:46:58.910
So now the loss is really tiny.

00:46:58.910 --> 00:47:00.710
So that's telling me
that of all the frames

00:47:00.710 --> 00:47:03.950
that I've captured, very,
very-- it's built a model where

00:47:03.950 --> 00:47:07.700
it can predict like
99.999% accuracy,

00:47:07.700 --> 00:47:10.830
it thinks, that if I give
it a frame, of what that is.

00:47:10.830 --> 00:47:13.580
So let's put that in action
and try playing "Pac-Man."

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:15.530
So I'm going to play.

00:47:15.530 --> 00:47:18.000
And I'm going to
point to the right.

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:20.649
Come on, Pac-Man.

00:47:20.649 --> 00:47:21.940
There, he's going to the right.

00:47:21.940 --> 00:47:22.540
I'll point up.

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:23.180
He's going up.

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:26.575
Ah, down, down, down.

00:47:26.575 --> 00:47:28.200
oh, it was this hand
I used, wasn't it?

00:47:28.200 --> 00:47:30.180
Down-- there we go.

00:47:30.180 --> 00:47:31.720
I forgot I changed hands.

00:47:31.720 --> 00:47:33.850
And now if I-- ah, he got me.

00:47:33.850 --> 00:47:35.387
But even you'll
see the highlights

00:47:35.387 --> 00:47:37.970
if you don't look-- if you look
at the highlight on the images

00:47:37.970 --> 00:47:38.469
itself.

00:47:38.469 --> 00:47:42.007
And you'll see it's actually
detecting, there's a right.

00:47:42.007 --> 00:47:43.590
This is hard, because
I have to switch

00:47:43.590 --> 00:47:45.170
hands, that kind of thing
that narrows it down.

00:47:45.170 --> 00:47:46.253
Come on, go left, Pac-Man.

00:47:46.253 --> 00:47:46.790
Eat.

00:47:46.790 --> 00:47:47.410
Go, go, go.

00:47:47.410 --> 00:47:48.880
There we go.

00:47:48.880 --> 00:47:51.330
Whoops.

00:47:51.330 --> 00:47:53.995
Ah, I'm going to die.

00:47:53.995 --> 00:47:54.620
Quickly switch.

00:47:54.620 --> 00:47:56.865
No, too late.

00:47:56.865 --> 00:47:58.490
But as you can see
here, this is just--

00:47:58.490 --> 00:47:59.810
this is entirely open source.

00:47:59.810 --> 00:48:01.170
So if you want to play
with the code yourself--

00:48:01.170 --> 00:48:02.990
we can switch back
to slides, please.

00:48:02.990 --> 00:48:06.890
So what I've done here is I
just captured those images live.

00:48:06.890 --> 00:48:09.177
I trained the model with
those images in the browser.

00:48:09.177 --> 00:48:10.760
You can see it only
took a few seconds

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:12.440
to get a reasonable accuracy.

00:48:12.440 --> 00:48:16.070
And then we ended up being able
to play "Pac-Man" really badly.

00:48:16.070 --> 00:48:18.737
OK, so finally then, the
last part to think about--

00:48:18.737 --> 00:48:21.320
and particularly thinking about
the future now is a real-world

00:48:21.320 --> 00:48:22.640
machine learning system--

00:48:22.640 --> 00:48:24.320
is that all this stuff that
I've been talking about,

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:26.111
building models and
all that kind of thing,

00:48:26.111 --> 00:48:28.100
is that tiny little
box in the middle when

00:48:28.100 --> 00:48:30.030
it comes to production.

00:48:30.030 --> 00:48:31.520
So when it comes--

00:48:31.520 --> 00:48:33.900
to be honest, as an industry,
we're still in the infancy.

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:35.780
There's still lots of
opportunity out there.

00:48:35.780 --> 00:48:37.730
And our ML code that we've
built is that little box

00:48:37.730 --> 00:48:38.390
in the middle.

00:48:38.390 --> 00:48:40.310
There's all these other things
that you're going to need.

00:48:40.310 --> 00:48:42.150
And I'm going to focus on
the ones on the right here,

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:44.520
which are the serving
infrastructure and monitoring.

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:45.710
So if you want to
build something that's

00:48:45.710 --> 00:48:47.915
production-ready, of course you
need a serving infrastructure

00:48:47.915 --> 00:48:49.730
that will allow you
to do it at scale.

00:48:49.730 --> 00:48:52.021
You need monitoring to make
sure you're doing it right,

00:48:52.021 --> 00:48:53.290
and all that kind of stuff.

00:48:53.290 --> 00:48:55.280
And so I would
recommend you go take

00:48:55.280 --> 00:48:57.290
a look at TensorFlow Serving.

00:48:57.290 --> 00:49:01.280
So just a few weeks ago, we
released TensorFlow Serving.

00:49:01.280 --> 00:49:03.650
The idea, it's a
server infrastructure

00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:06.110
for deploying models
and allowing people

00:49:06.110 --> 00:49:09.630
to access your models via the
web in a scalable and secure

00:49:09.630 --> 00:49:10.430
way.

00:49:10.430 --> 00:49:13.280
And the idea is keep
your traditional server

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:17.270
architecture, keep your
traditional request-response

00:49:17.270 --> 00:49:18.650
model.

00:49:18.650 --> 00:49:21.830
The demo that I just
showed, of "Pac-Man,"

00:49:21.830 --> 00:49:25.220
that actually downloaded
an existing model,

00:49:25.220 --> 00:49:27.680
MobileNet model, of
image classification.

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:30.530
And then I retrained the final
layer for those four classes.

00:49:30.530 --> 00:49:32.330
But the original model
that was downloaded

00:49:32.330 --> 00:49:34.280
that does most of the
work was something

00:49:34.280 --> 00:49:36.410
that got served to me out
of TensorFlow Serving.

00:49:36.410 --> 00:49:39.950
And as a result, I can actually
be able to deploy that model

00:49:39.950 --> 00:49:41.360
and retrain it as we just did.

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:44.120
So take a look at
TF Serving for that.

00:49:44.120 --> 00:49:46.310
So with that, this is
the theme that we're

00:49:46.310 --> 00:49:48.440
working on for TensorFlow.

00:49:48.440 --> 00:49:51.290
We're all about really trying
to make machines intelligent

00:49:51.290 --> 00:49:52.560
and improve people's lives.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:54.770
We've open-sourced this so
that you can build on it.

00:49:54.770 --> 00:49:57.140
Think of the scenarios that
you couldn't previously

00:49:57.140 --> 00:49:58.310
do as a programmer.

00:49:58.310 --> 00:50:00.940
Think of the things that you
can't do with if-then code.

00:50:00.940 --> 00:50:03.050
And you may be able to
do that with data in,

00:50:03.050 --> 00:50:05.162
answers in, being able
to infer rules out.

00:50:05.162 --> 00:50:07.370
So with that, I just want
to say thank you very much.

00:50:07.370 --> 00:50:08.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:08.870 --> 00:50:12.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

